WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.081
Symbolic repair tools apply

2
00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:04.654
fixed rewrite rules: they are precise

3
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.501
but they fail on shapes the

4
00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:07.820
rules do not cover.

5
00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.633
A language model can rewrite any regex

6
00:00:10.696 --> 00:00:12.591
but it often breaks the syntax

7
00:00:12.654 --> 00:00:14.170
or changes the language.

8
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.480
LRR, from EACL 2026, combines the two.

9
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.868
A symbolic tool, RegexScalpel

10
00:00:21.868 --> 00:00:23.566
finds the part at fault

11
00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:25.332
and the instructions tell

12
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.030
the model to rewrite it.

13
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.273
The paper reports a repair rate

14
00:00:29.334 --> 00:00:31.533
fifteen point four percentage points

15
00:00:31.594 --> 00:00:33.060
above the previous best.

16
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.816
In the task, the model is fixed

17
00:00:35.816 --> 00:00:37.176
Qwen three eight B

18
00:00:37.244 --> 00:00:39.760
with greedy decoding and at most five

19
00:00:39.828 --> 00:00:41.800
hundred twelve output tokens.

20
00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:43.964
The agent controls only the

21
00:00:44.032 --> 00:00:45.510
words the model reads.

22
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.246
The prompt receives the regex and the

23
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.180
parts RegexScalpel localized.

24
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.492
The task removes the function that

25
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:53.992
renders one part as text

26
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.070
and leaves the two prompt templates

27
00:00:56.127 --> 00:00:57.570
for the agent to rewrite.

28
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.470
Here is our version of that function.

29
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.194
When it knows a rewrite

30
00:01:02.251 --> 00:01:03.911
it checks that the rewrite is

31
00:01:03.969 --> 00:01:05.572
safe and keeps the language

32
00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:07.060
and offers it ready-made.

33
00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.139
For a-plus, repeated, it says

34
00:01:10.139 --> 00:01:12.050
replace with a-plus.

35
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.765
Otherwise it names the shape of the problem

36
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.800
and leaves the rewrite to the model.

37
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.551
The last part here

38
00:01:18.551 --> 00:01:21.280
a-plus then an optional b, repeated

39
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.451
is ambiguous, but no simple rule fixes it

40
00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:26.590
that case goes to the model.

41
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.010
How is a repair judged?

42
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.784
There are one hundred vulnerable regexes

43
00:01:31.845 --> 00:01:33.360
from the Polyglot corpus.

44
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.934
A repair is credited when it compiles

45
00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:37.866
the attack tool finds no attack

46
00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:39.798
and it still shares some strings

47
00:01:39.856 --> 00:01:40.910
with the original.

48
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.820
Then the two languages are

49
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:44.020
compared both ways

50
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.300
with one hundred random strings each.

51
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.750
Kept is the share of the original's

52
00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.730
strings that the repair matches.

53
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.259
Admitted is the share of the repair's

54
00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:55.320
strings that the original matches.

55
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.150
A credited repair scores

56
00:01:57.212 --> 00:01:58.630
the smaller of the two.

57
00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.477
Pause and predict.

58
00:02:00.547 --> 00:02:02.236
The widened repair keeps

59
00:02:02.306 --> 00:02:03.854
every original string

60
00:02:03.854 --> 00:02:06.810
but admits only thirty-four in a hundred.

61
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.440
What does it score?

62
00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:13.750
Zero point three four.

63
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.046
The mean of the two rates would

64
00:02:16.109 --> 00:02:17.616
be zero point six seven

65
00:02:17.616 --> 00:02:19.060
and hide the widening.

66
00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.531
A repair that is too narrow keeps

67
00:02:21.595 --> 00:02:23.330
just thirteen in a hundred.

68
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.336
To pass, a run must beat the human reference

69
00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:29.730
on every workload, on eight counts

70
00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.656
how much it repairs, how little it changes

71
00:02:32.656 --> 00:02:35.050
and how well it keeps the language.

72
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.840
No model passed.

73
00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.315
Twelve of sixteen scored above the

74
00:02:39.378 --> 00:02:41.190
reference's nine point seven.

75
00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.457
DeepSeek V4.1 Flash one oh

76
00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.184
one seven scored best, seventy-four

77
00:02:46.184 --> 00:02:48.838
and repaired three regexes in four

78
00:02:48.838 --> 00:02:51.860
but its regexes grew by half on average.

79
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.296
GPT-6.1 Terra stayed closest

80
00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.232
to the original language

81
00:02:56.232 --> 00:02:58.690
but repaired only forty percent.

82
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.480
Claude Opus 5.5 scored twenty-nine.

83
00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.561
So: localize with a symbolic tool

84
00:03:05.561 --> 00:03:07.620
and rewrite with the model.

85
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.931
The score takes the smaller

86
00:03:10.004 --> 00:03:11.529
of kept and admitted

87
00:03:11.529 --> 00:03:13.490
so widening costs in full.

88
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.322
And repairing more pulls

89
00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:16.742
against changing less

90
00:03:16.742 --> 00:03:19.520
the best score came with the largest growth.

91
00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.942
As an exercise

92
00:03:20.942 --> 00:03:23.586
add a rule for neighbouring repeats

93
00:03:23.586 --> 00:03:25.230
such as a-star a-star.

94
00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.943
Next comes Progent

95
00:03:26.943 --> 00:03:29.740
which guards an agent that uses tools.
